Both provide real, standard Bluetooth Core Specification structure/opcode
definitions and are used directly by bt_device_role.c / bt_l2cap_device_role.c
rather than reimplemented from memory.

These implement BlueRetro's Bluetooth Classic *host*-role stack (the ESP32 acting
as a Bluetooth Classic controller connecting outward to gamepads). They are
included unmodified as reference material: the HCI opcode usage, packet framing,
and L2CAP/SDP handling patterns are structurally relevant to the new device-role
(peripheral) code in bt_device_role/, which is original code written for this
project and is NOT a modification of BlueRetro's files.

BlueRetro itself was archived by its author on 2025-12-14. See its README for
the author's own notice inviting forks under the license.

All other code in this repository is original, written from public Bluetooth
Core Specification opcodes and the WiiBrew wiki's Wiimote protocol documentation
(https://wiibrew.org/wiki/Wiimote), and is licensed under Apache-2.0 as well
unless stated otherwise in a given file's header.

Additionally: several specific facts used in bt_device_role.c, bt_l2cap_device_role.c,
and bt_bdaddr_setup.c were identified by reading (not copying) the source of
rnconrad/WiimoteEmulator (https://github.com/rnconrad/WiimoteEmulator), a
project independently confirmed by its own users to pair successfully with
real Wii and vWii consoles. That repository has no LICENSE file, so no code
from it has been copied into this repository verbatim -- the facts below were
re-derived and re-implemented from scratch based on understanding *why* that
project's code does what it does, not by reproducing its expression:
  - The requirement that the emulated device's Bluetooth address carry a
    Nintendo OUI (their adapter.c checks/enforces this before anything else)
  - The specific Class of Device value real Wiimotes advertise (0x002504)
  - The requirement to explicitly disable Simple Secure Pairing mode, since
    the Wii only speaks legacy PIN-based pairing
  - Confirmation that the expected pairing PIN is the peer device's own
    Bluetooth address (their PIN callback plugin is a four-line function
    doing exactly this)
These are properties of the real Wii/Wiimote's behavior -- facts about a
third-party protocol -- not expressive content belonging to that project.
